WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
UDP is often chosen because it is simple, fast, and message-oriented.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:14.040
A sender can transmit a datagram without setting up a long-lived connection, and a receiver can process each datagram as a separate unit.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:21.980
That simplicity is useful, but it also means the application has to provide many of the safety properties that a higher-level protocol might otherwise define.

4
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:29.940
A UDP listener may receive traffic from peers that are unauthenticated, unexpected, misconfigured, outdated, or simply noisy.

5
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:40.360
The service may see malformed datagrams, duplicated datagrams, delayed datagrams, reordered datagrams, oversized input, or messages that belong to a different component.

6
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.260
The code should treat these as normal possibilities.

7
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:49.140
Local networks, private networks, and discovery environments should not be treated as automatically safe.

8
00:00:49.240 --> 00:01:00.760
A service may be reachable by other hosts, containers, test tools, monitoring systems, legacy clients, or devices that do not share the same trust assumptions.

9
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.300
Internal reachability is not the same as authorization.

10
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.620
Secure UDP starts with clear assumptions.

11
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.480
Who is allowed to send messages?

12
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.580
Which datagram types are accepted?

13
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.880
What source information is used only for routing, and what information is used for trust?

14
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.420
Which actions are allowed before stronger authentication or protocol validation succeeds?

15
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.000
Those assumptions should be explicit.

16
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:34.400
UDP defects can cause data exposure, unintended actions, corrupted state, service instability, or broken separation between components.

17
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:46.980
Defensive UDP code validates each datagram, constrains endpoint exposure, limits work and response size, handles loss and repetition, and treats availability as part of the security design.
